this month marks the 50th anniversary of  the signing of title ix which outlawed  sex-based discrimination in education in  sports even before its passage women had  already started making their push for  equality in the sporting world like on  june 3rd 1972 when the first ever  all-women road race was held right here  in new york city the 50th running of  that race now the mastercard new york  mini 10k is set to get underway in  central park this morning a race that's  grown from less than 100 women running  to a field of elite and amateur runners  and more than 200 000 finishers to date  i had a chance to look back on how the  mini 10k started and why it's more than  just another weekend race  what do you love about running  well i love the fact that it gets me  away from the trivialities of a day 75  years old and catherine switzer is still  running 40 miles a week that's because  running isn't just something she does  it's a part of who she is when did you  start running and knowing that it was  different for you  when i was 12 my dad said you don't want  to be a cheerleader in high school you  want people to cheer for you you should  go out and start running and make the  field hockey team which is a new thing  in your high school and i began running  every day and it gave me such a sense of  empowerment i felt like i had a victory  under my belt every day nobody could  take away from me the year was 1959  a time when most young girls weren't  encouraged to run or play sports but  rather discouraged i remember the  milkman coming to my mother and saying  your little girl okay i see her out  running and she said no no no she's fine  and then my girlfriends who are  concerned you know you're going to get  big legs you're going to grow hair on  your chest you're never going to have  children all of these myths they really  believe people said that they said that  to me  doctors said that to me definitely my  uterus was going to fall out and i  really should be careful  it was so ridiculous switzer's  determination to run became front page  news in  the 1967 most famous foot race even  attracts a leggy lady just 20 years old  she entered the boston marathon at the  time women weren't allowed there was  nothing about gender on the entry form  and my coach said you have to enter the  race you don't count if you don't enter  the race and so i entered the race but i  signed my name k.v switzer that's how i  got the bib number but in the race with  the bib number the official jumped off  the officials bus furious screamed at me  get the hell out of my race and give me  those numbers and tried to rip the  numbers off of me my coach couldn't get  him away but my burly boyfriend  knocked the official out of the race  instead if i don't finish this race  nobody's going to believe women can do  it so i decided to finish the race and  that changed my life and running  five years later in 1972  women were officially allowed to enter  the boston marathon the publicity led to  more opportunities for women like the  first ever all women's road race with  johnson wax's crazy leg shave cream the  sponsor they thought wouldn't it be cool  to have a women's only marathon in  central park well there are only about  eight of us who could run a marathon  so switzer fellow elite runner nina  kousik and fred lebo the founder of the  new york city marathon came up with the  idea of one lap of central park six  miles or ten thousand meters the crazy  legs mini marathon  a name also inspired by fashion of the  time the mini skirt so the race then  became also an emblem of women's  equality  and  feistiness and freedom because the  miniskirt was that emblem and now we had  our very own race but not without some  challenges the biggest challenge was  convincing women to come  and and really yes the biggest challenge  was because they maybe jogged a mile or  two a day but now this was six miles and  that really scared them we sent out so  many entries we had 78 women show up and  with that i was gonna at the time that  was a huge number it was huge we said  it's going to be a success and it was it  really was did it feel  that you had to compromise in some ways  to get this also or no well we didn't  feel we had to compromise but fred  lebeau was really an operator and he was  trying anything to get publicity and he  would do things like go to the playboy  bunny club and recruit playboy bunnies  to come for a photo op now that we  didn't really like to promote the race  right yeah but you know we all said look  fred just wants the publicity right  however and if we get to run yeah when  the gun went off it was a serious race  switzer still has the t-shirt she wore  in the inaugural race 50 years later i  can still get into it and while the race  no longer has its crazy legs designation  now the new york mini 10k there is still  a nod to the original name thanks to  runners like julianne grace and her  daughter dede beck  the two are crazy legs runners meaning  they have at least 15 new york mini 10ks  under their belts what makes you go back  here absolutely incredible i think you  know just that feeling of sisterhood  with all these other women runners you  know 5 000 8 000 women out there  julianne has 45 finishes missing just  one after her first in 1975. i was very  nervous  because i hadn't run 10 kilometers and  then during the race i remember feeling  just  i'm doing this i'm doing this and  i was 195th  out of  276 finishers  i wasn't lost so that was pretty good  and i felt so great i felt i had done  something remarkable dede then followed  in her mother's footsteps and now has 42  straight finishes despite a recent  diagnosis of the muscle disease dystonia  i can still participate with the  crutches  and you know still be part of it did you  ever think this is too tough to do this  with a disability like this no i didn't  i ran it in 2019 with the dystonia and  with you know kind of tripping and  stumbling and slowly but i made it and  then 2020 it was canceled and since i  did it last year  i know i can do it this year after all  it's tradition with dee dee's three  daughters a third generation in the  family also running each june  generationally  to pass something like this on what does  that mean to you  it's hard to describe in words to have  my daughter follow along with something  i love  and to have  her daughters my three granddaughters to  have these girls show up for the mini it  makes me so proud  it makes me  feel loved  and ensures the impact of the new york  mini marathon will live on it's not just  about running it's about what women have  achieved  since the 1970s how much women have  achieved in education in careers in  athletics it's about women's progress  through the years i mean that's what it  means to me in many ways it really does  it's symbolic i hope to do it every year  i can that's why i show up  that's a good reason so how about this  for progress women now make up more than  half the runners out there that started  in 2019  it's an amazing thing to see the number  of women that come out year after year  catherine switzer is going to be wearing  that shirt she still had today at the  race which is getting underway and then  also she told me this is what inspired  her to then push to get women in the  olympics in the marathon which was joan  benoit which ended up being what 12  years later after this so the push that  they have made and that the new york  road runners have made by keeping this  race when they didn't have sponsors at  certain times it's just really critical  to be a reminder of where we were and  where we are now katherine's sister's  something else  had a chance to talk to her about 10  years ago but it's her poise and her  absolute determination that has gotten  us to where we are today so excited for  her why are you not running via live  shots yeah or you could be broadcasting  from the race that's what we would like  to see  i was in boston last night yeah oh we  won't talk about that but that is a good  idea
 

